2. WHY PARLIAMENT MATTERS

SSC General Awareness frequently tests direct constitutional facts.

The important thing is not memorizing 100 Articles.

You need to understand the structure first.

Think of India like this:

                    PRESIDENT
                       |
             ---------------------
             |                   |
        LOK SABHA           RAJYA SABHA
       House of People      Council of States
             \                   /
              \                 /
                PARLIAMENT
Most important fact

Parliament of India =

President + Lok Sabha + Rajya Sabha

This is a very common conceptual trap.

Many students incorrectly think:

Parliament = Lok Sabha + Rajya Sabha

❌ Incomplete.

The President is also a component of Parliament.

3. WHAT IS LOK SABHA?

Lok Sabha = House of the People

Think:

LOK = PEOPLE

Therefore:

Lok Sabha represents the people directly.

Members are directly elected by the people.

Simple example

Suppose:

People
 ↓
Vote
 ↓
Elect representatives
 ↓
Lok Sabha

That's why Lok Sabha is called the popular house.

4. WHAT IS RAJYA SABHA?

Rajya Sabha = Council of States

Think:

RAJYA = STATE

It represents the States and Union Territories in the Union Parliament.

Members are generally elected indirectly by elected members of State Legislative Assemblies, using proportional representation by means of the single transferable vote.

Don't overcomplicate this now.

Remember:

Lok Sabha
= People
= Direct election

Rajya Sabha
= States
= Mainly indirect election
5. BIGGEST DIFFERENCE
Feature	Lok Sabha	Rajya Sabha
Meaning	House of People	Council of States
Election	Direct	Mainly indirect
Represents	People	States/UTs
Maximum constitutional strength	550 elected members + up to 2 Anglo-Indian nominations historically, but nomination provision was removed	250
Current constitutional maximum	550 elected	250
Minimum age	25	30
Term	Normally 5 years	Permanent House
Dissolution	Can be dissolved	Cannot be dissolved
Presiding officer	Speaker	Vice-President as Chairman
Money Bill	Major role	Limited role
⚠️ Important 2026 point

Do not memorize the old statement:

"Lok Sabha has 552 members."

That came from the old constitutional provision allowing nomination of two Anglo-Indian members.

That nomination provision was discontinued after the constitutional amendment.

For exam purposes:

Maximum elected Lok Sabha seats = 550

And:

Rajya Sabha maximum = 250

6. WHY IS RAJYA SABHA CALLED PERMANENT?

This is extremely important.

Rajya Sabha is a:

Permanent House

It is never dissolved.

But that does NOT mean members stay forever.

Instead:

Approximately one-third of members retire every two years.

Each Rajya Sabha member normally has a:

6-year term

So:

Rajya Sabha
     |
Permanent House
     |
1/3 members retire
every 2 years
     |
New members replace them
Memory trick

Rajya = Remains

Rajya Sabha remains.

7. LOK SABHA TERM

Normally:

5 years

But it can be dissolved earlier.

Example:

Lok Sabha formed
      ↓
5-year normal term
      ↓
Election
      ↓
New Lok Sabha

There are constitutional provisions concerning extension during a national emergency, but for SSC basic questions, remember:

Normal term = 5 years

8. MINIMUM AGE

This is a classic SSC question.

Lok Sabha

Minimum age:

25 years

Rajya Sabha

Minimum age:

30 years

Memory trick
LOK = 25
RAJYA = 30

Or:

Lower House → Lower age

9. WHO IS THE SPEAKER?

The Speaker presides over Lok Sabha.

Think:

LOK SABHA
    ↓
SPEAKER

The Speaker is elected by the Lok Sabha from among its members.

10. WHO PRESIDES OVER RAJYA SABHA?

The Vice-President of India is the:

ex-officio Chairman of Rajya Sabha

"Ex-officio" means:

A person holds a position because of another office they already hold.

So:

Vice-President
      ↓
Ex-officio
      ↓
Chairman of Rajya Sabha
Very important distinction

Rajya Sabha Chairman = Vice-President

But:

Rajya Sabha Deputy Chairman = elected by Rajya Sabha

11. PRIME MINISTER'S ROLE

The Prime Minister is normally the:

Leader of the majority in Lok Sabha / leader of the majority party or coalition commanding its confidence.

The Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to:

Lok Sabha

This is an extremely important fact.

Why?

Because India follows a parliamentary system.

The government must maintain the confidence of the elected House.

Therefore:

Council of Ministers → collectively responsible to Lok Sabha

Not Rajya Sabha.

12. PRESIDENT + PARLIAMENT

Remember:

Parliament
   |
   |--- President
   |
   |--- Lok Sabha
   |
   |--- Rajya Sabha

The President is therefore an integral part of Parliament, although the President is not a member of either House.

That's a common trap.

13. MONEY BILL

Now we reach one of the highest-value Parliament topics.

A Money Bill is introduced:

Only in Lok Sabha

And:

Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend a Money Bill.

Rajya Sabha can make recommendations.

Lok Sabha may accept or reject those recommendations.

14. MONEY BILL — 14-DAY RULE

Rajya Sabha must return a Money Bill within:

14 days

If Rajya Sabha doesn't return it within 14 days:

It is deemed to have been passed by both Houses in the form passed by Lok Sabha.

Memory

Money = 14

Don't confuse:

Lok Sabha normal term → 5 years
Rajya Sabha member term → 6 years
Money Bill → 14 days
15. WHO DECIDES WHETHER A BILL IS A MONEY BILL?

The:

Speaker of Lok Sabha

certifies whether a bill is a Money Bill.

This is an important SSC trap.

Not:

❌ President
❌ Prime Minister
❌ Rajya Sabha Chairman

Correct:

✅ Speaker of Lok Sabha

16. ORDINARY BILL vs MONEY BILL
Feature	Ordinary Bill	Money Bill
Can originate	Either House	Only Lok Sabha
Rajya Sabha role	Strong	Limited
Can Rajya Sabha amend?	Yes	Only recommend
President's assent	Required	Required
Speaker certification	No special certification	Yes
Rajya Sabha time limit	No 14-day Money Bill rule	14 days
17. WHAT HAPPENS IF LOK SABHA AND RAJYA SABHA DISAGREE?

For an ordinary bill, there can be a:

Joint sitting of Parliament

Joint sitting is presided over by:

Speaker of Lok Sabha

But remember:

Money Bill

❌ No joint sitting.

This is a very useful trap.

18. PRESIDENTIAL ASSENT

After Parliament passes a bill, it goes to the President.

The President can:

give assent
withhold assent in certain circumstances
return an ordinary bill for reconsideration

But a Money Bill cannot be returned for reconsideration in the same manner.

